              "type": "text",
              "text": "Crosslink.lean — the sealed source"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "json",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Crosslink.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Crosslink.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 6,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-a_tree_uses_one_link_per_leaf_and_no_more",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE SPARSEST CONNECTED SHAPE THERE IS. A tree over n leaves carries exactly n − 1 links — one per child, none spare — while the same n leaves admit n(n − 1)/2 pairs. Decided across leaf counts from 4 to 1024: the tree's link count never reaches the pair count once n exceeds three, and the gap widens quadratically. The tree is not a small graph, it is the smallest one that still connects."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "[4,8,16,17,20,32,64,128,256,1024].all (fun n => (n - 1 <= (n * (n - 1)) / 2) && ((n <= 3) || (n - 1 < (n * (n - 1)) / 2)))"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem a_tree_uses_one_link_per_leaf_and_no_more : [4,8,16,17,20,32,64,128,256,1024].all (fun n => (n - 1 <= (n * (n - 1)) / 2) && ((n <= 3) || (n - 1 < (n * (n - 1)) / 2))) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-the_measured_store_uses_a_vanishing_share",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE STORE AS IT STANDS, WALKED RATHER THAN QUOTED. 71,719 leaves, 71,718 parent links, 2,571,771,621 pairs available — the tree uses one link for every 35,859 pairs its own leaves already permit. Stated as an inequality with a factor rather than a percentage, because a percentage rounds and this ledger decides: the available pairs exceed the tree's links by more than two thousand times."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(2571771621 = (71719 * (71719 - 1)) / 2) ∧ (2571771621 > 2000 * 71718)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem the_measured_store_uses_a_vanishing_share : (2571771621 = (71719 * (71719 - 1)) / 2) ∧ (2571771621 > 2000 * 71718) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-crosslinking_outgrows_the_address_space",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE CLAIM THE CAPTAIN NAMED, DECIDED AS A COMPARISON OF EXPONENTS. E possible edges admit 2^E graphs, and the address space is 2^128. So the graph space exceeds the address space exactly when E > 128, and E = n(n − 1)/2 passes 128 at n = 17 — seventeen leaves. Decided for every tabulated n from 17 upward. A base-2 power is monotone in its exponent, so comparing exponents settles the powers; stating 2^(2^63) directly would be a number no kernel can check, which is a claim wearing arithmetic rather than doing it."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "([17,20,32,64,128,256,1024].all (fun n => (n * (n - 1)) / 2 > 128)) ∧ ((16 * 15) / 2 <= 128)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem crosslinking_outgrows_the_address_space : ([17,20,32,64,128,256,1024].all (fun n => (n * (n - 1)) / 2 > 128)) ∧ ((16 * 15) / 2 <= 128) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-seventeen_leaves_already_pass_the_whole_uuid",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE THRESHOLD IS SEVENTEEN, AND IT IS EXACT. At sixteen leaves the pairs number 120, which is under 128; at seventeen they number 136, which is over. So a crosslink graph on SEVENTEEN handle folders already admits more configurations than the entire uuid space holds addresses — with 2³² folders available. The address space is the floor of this structure and not its ceiling, and the floor is passed before a store has eighteen entries."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((16 * 15) / 2 = 120) ∧ ((17 * 16) / 2 = 136) ∧ (120 <= 128) ∧ (136 > 128)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem seventeen_leaves_already_pass_the_whole_uuid : ((16 * 15) / 2 = 120) ∧ ((17 * 16) / 2 = 136) ∧ (120 <= 128) ∧ (136 > 128) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-the_leaf_is_not_one_uuid_but_a_folder",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "AND THE FILES INSIDE ARE PLURAL, MEASURED. Of 71,719 leaves, 710 carry no theorem key, 70,929 carry 1 key, 75 carry 2 keys, 5 carry 3 keys — 80 carry more than one, so a handle folder is a folder and not a synonym for a single uuid, and the counts sum to the leaf total exactly. A store where every leaf held exactly one thing would have no interior to crosslink; this one does."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(710 + 70929 + 75 + 5 = 71719) ∧ (80 > 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem the_leaf_is_not_one_uuid_but_a_folder : (710 + 70929 + 75 + 5 = 71719) ∧ (80 > 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-three_kinds_partition_the_store",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE FOLDERS ARE NOT ALL THE SAME THING EITHER: 71009 chunk, 448 page, 262 publication, summing to 71,719 exactly — no leaf counted twice and none left out. A crosslink graph over a store with kinds is a graph with typed nodes, which is a different and larger object than a graph over one kind; sealing the partition first is what makes that statement meaningful rather than decorative."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "71009 + 448 + 262 = 71719"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem three_kinds_partition_the_store : 71009 + 448 + 262 = 71719 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
